        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains under acute operational stress. The sharpest development in the past six hours is the IRGC's claimed interception of a drone over the strait, carried independently by three outlets and reported here without further qualification pending official confirmation from other parties. Separately, our AIS tracking shows 361 vessels holding position away from berth as of 14:52 UTC, a metric that excludes ships within 25 km of a working port. Port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides reached 643 in the most recent 24 hours. PortWatch's most recent published day, 2026-07-23, logged 10 transits against a pre-crisis baseline of 88 per day; a multi-day lag is inherent to that weekly dataset. Brent crude printed at $83.45 as of 14:52 UTC, a move of negative 5.09% over 24 hours. The Crisis Pressure composite sits in the extreme band, flat over the past day, with physical transit deviation as the top contributor. The Escalation Forecast composite sits in the elevated band and has been easing, driven primarily by Polymarket escalation contracts; those two composites diverge, measuring present disruption and forward risk respectively. On the diplomatic track, Iran has denied current direct talks with Washington, while unverified single-source reports point to Iran-Oman discussions over a possible shipping corridor arrangement.",
